2025-05-01 03:17:38 - Thread-1308 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns and why they matter  
• cities : city_name  – we need the city’s name  
• cities : country_code_2 – to filter only China (country_code_2 = ‘cn’)  
• cities : insert_date – to restrict to July‑2021 and to study which dates appear, consecutively or not  

No other table stores daily city‑level “insert_date” values, so all queries will read from cities only.

Below are 10 exploratory → analytic queries (each limited to 20 rows) that progressively build the logic needed to discover (1) the longest and (2) the shortest streak of consecutive July‑2021 dates for Chinese cities.  
(Every query is preceded by an explanatory comment.)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few Chinese cities to confirm the spelling format.
SELECT DISTINCT city_name
FROM cities
WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. See which July‑2021 dates exist for China and how many rows per date.
SELECT insert_date     AS date,
       COUNT(*)        AS rows_on_date
FROM cities
WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
  AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
GROUP BY insert_date
ORDER BY insert_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. List every Chinese July‑2021 row, sorted by date then city.
SELECT insert_date, city_name
FROM cities
WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
  AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
ORDER BY insert_date, city_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Produce the ordered distinct dates only (to prepare for streak logic).
SELECT DISTINCT insert_date
FROM cities
WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
  AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
ORDER BY insert_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Attach a running row_number() to each date (SQLite window function).
SELECT insert_date,
       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY insert_date) AS rn
FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT insert_date
    FROM cities
    WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
      AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
) AS d
ORDER BY insert_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Derive a “group id” for every date:  julianday(date) – rn
--    Same value -> same consecutive streak.  We will later count sizes.
WITH d AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT insert_date
    FROM cities
    WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
      AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
), x AS (
    SELECT insert_date,
           ROW_NUMBER()      OVER (ORDER BY insert_date)               AS rn,
           julianday(insert_date) - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY insert_date) AS grp
    FROM d
)
SELECT * FROM x
ORDER BY insert_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Count how many days are contained in each consecutive‑date group.
WITH d AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT insert_date
    FROM cities
    WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
      AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
), x AS (
    SELECT insert_date,
           julianday(insert_date) - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY insert_date) AS grp
    FROM d
)
SELECT grp,
       MIN(insert_date) AS start_date,
       MAX(insert_date) AS end_date,
       COUNT(*)         AS streak_len
FROM x
GROUP BY grp
ORDER BY streak_len DESC, start_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Identify the maximum streak length found.
WITH d AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT insert_date
    FROM cities
    WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
      AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
), x AS (
    SELECT insert_date,
           julianday(insert_date) - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY insert_date) AS grp
    FROM d
), g AS (
    SELECT grp, COUNT(*) AS streak_len
    FROM x
    GROUP BY grp
)
SELECT MAX(streak_len) AS longest_streak
FROM g;
```

```sql
-- 9. Identify the minimum (shortest) streak length.
WITH d AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT insert_date
    FROM cities
    WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
      AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
), x AS (
    SELECT insert_date,
           julianday(insert_date) - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY insert_date) AS grp
    FROM d
), g AS (
    SELECT grp, COUNT(*) AS streak_len
    FROM x
    GROUP BY grp
)
SELECT MIN(streak_len) AS shortest_streak
FROM g;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final query: return one (capitalized) city for every date that belongs to
--     (a) the longest streak and (b) the shortest streak – ordered by date.
WITH chinese_july AS (
    SELECT insert_date,
           city_name,
           julianday(insert_date) - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY insert_date) AS grp
    FROM (
        SELECT DISTINCT insert_date, city_name
        FROM cities
        WHERE country_code_2 = 'cn'
          AND insert_date BETWEEN '2021-07-01' AND '2021-07-31'
    )
), streak_sizes AS (
    SELECT grp, COUNT(*) AS streak_len
    FROM chinese_july
    GROUP BY grp
), extremes AS (
    SELECT grp
    FROM streak_sizes
    WHERE streak_len = (SELECT MAX(streak_len) FROM streak_sizes)
       OR streak_len = (SELECT MIN(streak_len) FROM streak_sizes)
)
SELECT cj.insert_date          AS date,
       upper(substr(cj.city_name,1,1))
       || lower(substr(cj.city_name,2)) AS city_name
FROM chinese_july  cj
JOIN extremes      e  ON cj.grp = e.grp
GROUP BY cj.insert_date              -- exactly one record per date
ORDER BY cj.insert_date;
```

Run the queries step‑by‑step; the last one (`-- 10. Final query`) will produce the required result set: all dates for the shortest and longest consecutive July‑2021 streaks in China, each accompanied by one correctly‑capitalized city name.
